{
  "id": "k8s-impersonation",
  "provider": "kubernetes",
  "title": "Scoped impersonation for elevated access",
  "summary": "Engineers have read-only access by default. For an incident they run kubectl --as to act as a dedicated operator service account, and only that one, because the impersonate permission is restricted by resourceNames.",
  "notes": [
    "The impersonate verb lets a subject act as another user, group or service account. Without resourceNames it lets you become anyone, including system:masters, so treat it like cluster-admin.",
    "Audit logs record both the real user and the impersonated identity (impersonatedUser), so elevated actions stay attributable.",
    "Pair with time-bound membership in the group that holds the impersonate binding (e.g. a PIM-style just-in-time group in your IdP).",
    "Example: kubectl --as=system:serviceaccount:ops:incident-operator rollout restart deploy/api -n payments."
  ],
  "docs": [
    {
      "title": "User impersonation",
      "url": "https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/access-authn-authz/authentication/#user-impersonation"
    },
    {
      "title": "Auditing",
      "url": "https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/debug/debug-cluster/audit/"
    },
    {
      "title": "RBAC good practices",
      "url": "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/rbac-good-practices/"
    }
  ],
